:'''March 7th:''' I appreciate your kind words, General Huaiyan. But won't teaching me swordplay be a waste of Yanqing and Yunli's time? They should be preparing for the ceremony.
:'''March 7th:''' Plus, I heard that each swordmaster has their own special moves. What if they let something slip while teaching me? If everyone knows each other's tactics, won't that mean everyone is stuck in a stalemate and unable to end their fights during the Wardance?
:'''Yunli:''' That's considerate of you, March 7th. But don't worry. It'll take you at least a decade of hard training before you can even start learning special moves. No need to freak out – a few Zhuming swordplay tricks will mean you'll be more than equipped.
:'''March 7th:''' R&mdash;really?
:'''Dan Heng:''' Looks like March's curiosity has been piqued.
:'''Huaiyan:''' Heh, the whole point is to know each other's moves. Defeating your opponent in just one move? How boring would that be! Plus, what really decides a swordsman's fate isn't some "special move" &mdash; it's the solid fundamentals.
:'''Jing Yuan:''' So, Miss Yunli has already agreed. What do you say, Yanqing?
:'''Yanqing:''' General... I haven't graduated yet. How can I be qualified to teach swordplay to others?
:'''Yunli:''' So, you're admitting your defeat, huh? If you're not even confident to teach, why don't you let me be the ringmaster instead?
:'''Jing Yuan:''' Yanqing, teaching an apprentice is also a way of honing your own skills and gaining insights. You've been an apprentice for years, it's about time you looked at swordplay from another perspective.
:'''Yanqing:''' I see, General. Then count me in!
:'''Jing Yuan:''' Now that Yanqing has agreed too, it all comes down to Miss March giving her nod.
:'''Dan Heng:''' It's up to you to make the final decision, March.
:{{Диалог|Стрелка}} Time for you to become the best swordmaster on the Express.
::'''(Trailblazer):''' At least that way, I won't have to worry about you accidentally shooting me in the butt all the time.
